    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While the shareholder-only model is internally consistent based on 20th-century economics, it is increasingly obsolete because it ignores the systemic risks that material from the margins into the core of the value equation. History shows that ignoring social and environmental factors creates a perfect storm of regulatory capture, activist dissent, and a skewed global supply chain that can collapse a firm overnight, regardless of its short-term margins. \n\nConsequently, the strategy that was once the \"rational\" way to run a business has morphed into a dangerous vulnerability for any entity operating in the 21st century. To truly succeed today, leadership must evolve from a narrow focus on profit to a balanced approach that recognizes these \"externalities\" as actual internal revenue or loss drivers, ensuring the company survives not just financially, but socially.",
